@node Using Demo Notes
@chapter Using Demo Notes
@cindex note list

  A @dfn{note list} is a read-only buffer with one entry for each
@file{.txt} file in a directory.  Each entry shows the first line of
the file as its title.  An empty file uses its file name as the title.
The list does not include subdirectories or hidden files, whose names
begin with @samp{.}.

@menu
* Opening Notes::      Choose a directory and visit a note.
* Filtering: Filtering the Note List. Select notes by title.
* Ordering Notes::     Choose the order of the entries.
* Appearance::         Customize the appearance of titles.
@end menu

@node Opening Notes
@section Opening and Visiting Notes

  To work with notes in a directory, type @kbd{M-x demo-notes} and
enter the directory's name, represented here by @var{directory}:

@example
M-x demo-notes @key{RET} @var{directory} @key{RET}
@end example

@noindent
Replace @var{directory} with the name of an existing directory that
contains your note files.

@deffn Command demo-notes directory
Read the notes in @var{directory} and display them in a buffer using
Demo Notes List mode.  The command selects the window showing the
list.  When called interactively, it prompts for a directory, using
the current buffer's default directory as the default.

The directory must exist and be readable.  A directory with no note
files produces an empty list.
@end deffn

  The following keys are available in the list:

@table @kbd
@item @key{RET}
Visit the note on the current line (@code{demo-notes-visit}).

@item g
Read the note titles again (@code{demo-notes-refresh}).

@item /
Show notes with matching titles.  @xref{Filtering the Note List}.
@end table

@kindex RET @r{(Demo Notes list)}
@deffn Command demo-notes-visit
Visit the file for the entry at point in the selected window.  Signal
an error if point is not on an entry or if the file no longer exists.
This command is available only in a Demo Notes List buffer.
@end deffn

@kindex g @r{(Demo Notes list)}
@deffn Command demo-notes-refresh
Read the note titles again from the list's directory.  This command
is available only in a Demo Notes List buffer.  It keeps the current
filter and orders the entries according to
@code{demo-notes-sort-order}.  Point stays on the same entry if that
entry is still displayed; otherwise, it moves to the first entry.
If the list is empty, point moves to the beginning of the buffer.

Reading finishes before this command returns.  If reading fails or
you quit during reading, the previous list remains displayed.  After
a successful refresh, the command runs
@code{demo-notes-after-refresh-hook}.  @xref{Refresh Hooks}.
@end deffn

@node Filtering the Note List
@section Filtering the Note List
@cindex filtering notes
@cindex notes, filtering

  A filter selects the titles shown in the note list.  It does not
rename or modify note files.  Each list buffer has its own filter.

@kindex / @r{(Demo Notes list)}
@deffn Command demo-notes-filter string
Show only entries whose titles contain @var{string}, ignoring case.
The match is a literal substring match, not a regular expression.
When called interactively, this command reads @var{string} from the
minibuffer.  Empty input removes the current filter.  This command
is available only in a Demo Notes List buffer.

Filtering uses the entries already read into the list.  To include
changes made to note files, refresh the list first.
@end deffn

  For example, the filter @samp{meeting} matches both @samp{Meeting
notes} and @samp{Next meeting}.  If no titles match, the list is empty.
Remove the filter to show all entries again.

@node Ordering Notes
@section Choosing the Order of Notes
@cindex ordering notes

  A note list can be ordered by title or by modification time.  The
same ordering applies when a filter hides some entries.

@defopt demo-notes-sort-order
This option specifies how a note list orders its entries.  Its
default value is @code{title}.  The allowed values are:

@table @code
@item title
Sort by title, ignoring case.  Entries with equal titles are ordered
by file name.

@item newest
Show the most recently modified file first.  Files with the same
modification time are ordered by file name.
@end table

Changing this global option affects the next refresh of each list.
It does not change an existing list immediately.
@end defopt

  After loading Demo Notes, you can use Customize to save the order
for future sessions.  To try a different order in the current session,
evaluate this form and refresh the list:

@lisp
(setq demo-notes-sort-order 'newest)
@end lisp

@noindent
This setting lasts until you change it again or end the Emacs session.
For information about saving settings, see
@ref{Saving Customizations,,, emacs, The GNU Emacs Manual}.

@node Appearance
@section Customizing the Appearance of Titles
@cindex faces, note titles

  The note list uses a face for titles so that you can change their
appearance independently of the surrounding text.

@defvr Face demo-notes-title
This face displays note titles and inherits from @code{default}.
Customizing it changes titles in existing and future note lists.
@end defvr

@node Programming
@chapter Reading Notes from Lisp

  Lisp programs can read note entries without displaying a note list.
They can also respond after an existing list has been refreshed.

@menu
* Reading Entries::    Retrieve titles and file names.
* Refresh Hooks::      Act after a list has been refreshed.
@end menu

@node Reading Entries
@section Reading Note Entries

  The entry-reading function returns the same titles used by the note
list.  It does not select a window or change the current buffer.

@deftp {Data Type} demo-note title file
A note entry is a cons cell @code{(@var{title} . @var{file})}.
The @var{title} is a string containing the first line of the file,
without its newline; for an empty file, it is the file name without
its directory.  The @var{file} is an absolute file name.
@end deftp

@defun demo-notes-entries directory @
  &optional include-hidden
Return a newly allocated list of note entries for @var{directory},
which must be a string naming a readable directory.  Only regular
files with the extension @file{.txt} are included.  Subdirectories
are not searched.  If @var{include-hidden} is non-@code{nil}, include
files whose names begin with @samp{.}; otherwise, omit those files.

The returned entries are sorted by absolute file name, independently
of @code{demo-notes-sort-order}.  The function returns @code{nil} if
no files match.  It signals a @code{file-error} if the directory or
any matching file cannot be read.  It does not modify files or list
buffers.  Callers may modify the returned list and its strings.
@end defun

@node Refresh Hooks
@section Responding to a Refresh
@cindex hooks, refreshing the note list

  A program can use the refresh hook to update information derived
from a note list.  The hook runs only after the new entries have
replaced the previous list.

@defvar demo-notes-after-refresh-hook
This normal hook runs with the refreshed note list as the current
buffer, after point has been restored.  Its default value is
@code{nil}, and its functions receive no arguments.  Functions on
this hook may inspect the list but should not refresh it recursively.

The hook also runs when @code{demo-notes} first populates a list.
It does not run when reading fails or is canceled.  An error in a
hook function propagates to the caller; the refreshed list remains
in place.
@end defvar

  Use @code{add-hook} to register a function.  A buffer-local hook
value affects only that list buffer.  Remove a registered function
with @code{remove-hook} when it is no longer needed.
@xref{Hooks,,, emacs, The GNU Emacs Manual}.

